.TH GIT-NEW-WT 1
.SH NAME
git-new-wt \- create a Git worktree for a branch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git new-wt
.I branch-name
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git new-wt
creates a Git worktree for the specified branch.
.PP
The worktree is created alongside the main repository, inside a directory
with the same name as the main repository and the suffix
.IR __worktrees .
The worktree directory itself is named after the last component of the
branch name.
.PP
For example, the branch
.I feature/initial-release
in a repository named
.I project
is checked out into:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\&../project__worktrees/initial-release
.fi
.RE
.PP
The command handles the specified branch as follows:
.IP \(bu 2
If the branch exists locally, the worktree is created from the local branch.
.IP \(bu 2
If the branch does not exist locally but exists on
.IR origin ,
the remote branch is fetched and a local tracking branch is created for
the worktree.
.IP \(bu 2
If the branch does not exist locally or on
.IR origin ,
a new local branch is created. If the current branch is
.I main
or
.IR master ,
it is used as the base. Otherwise, the command offers to use
.I main
or
.I master
as the base when available. If neither is selected, the current branch
is used.
.PP
When a base branch tracks a remote branch, the command checks its upstream
status before creating the new branch and warns if the local branch is not
up to date.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a worktree for the branch
.IR docs/update-readme :
.PP
.RS
.nf
git new-wt docs/update-readme
.fi
.RE
.PP
Assuming the main repository is named
.IR project ,
the resulting layout is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
project/
project__worktrees/
    update-readme/
.fi
.RE
